THE DEFECT, at this commit. `thm` in docs/opentheory-importer-poc/OTPoC.lean
extended Γ handling from empty-only to non-empty, and checked the declared
hypotheses by COUNT alone (:992-993):

    unless th.hyps.size == ls.size do
      throwError "thm: declared Γ ({ls.size}) disagrees with the proof's open hypotheses ..."

`ls` — the article's declared hypothesis TERMS — was bound and then used only for
`.size`. The exported statement was built from the proof's own fvars, never from
`ls`. So an article could declare a sequent it had not proved, as long as the
COUNT matched, and the importer would mint a true, kernel-checked Lean theorem
whose stated antecedents were never established. Measured by the repairing lane on
an article declaring the FALSE sequent `q ⊢ p` over a proof of `p ⊢ p`: sizes
agreed, the check passed, success was logged, and `∀ p, p → p` was exported.

⚠ BLAST RADIUS, stated so it is not over-read: this is the PoC importer under
docs/opentheory-importer-poc/, not the production TCB, and nothing in the tree
builds it — which is also why nothing caught it.

FIXED in 40a59541b — "OpenTheory importer: an article declaring `q ⊢ p` minted a
Lean `p → p` and nothing could tell — Γ checked by CONTENT, the axiom gate made
exact, and the whole file wired to something that RUNS". At HEAD, `ls` feeds
`declaredΓ ← ls.mapM (fun o => o.asTerm)` and each declared hypothesis must be
defeq-matched against the proof's assumed Γ, with its own error
("the article asserts a sequent it did not prove").

